


News letter 2023

Hello everyone, this has been a long time waiting. While this season comes around, I would like to share with all of you my first newsletter. This has been a challenging year but definitely filled with long awaited events. As many of you have been a big part of my career progression over the seasons, I am thrilled to finally share with you some of these stories, memories, and of course my progression over the past year. 

As this may be the first time for some of you here is my introduction:
My name is Carlos Charabati, I love the water and am passionate about sailing. I've been sailing from the youngest age. Grew up like many through sailing school and at the sailing club. Today I thrive and work to sail at the highest level, and reach the top. Therefore this is my project.. 

Now time for an insight on this journey in the past year, this has definitely been the most accomplished year to date, featuring a top 6 finish at the world championships.
Let's begin our journey with a little overview of the past 9 months. Starting in June of 2023, finishing off with a strong season build up, I was getting ready for the peak events coming up:

Ilca 6 world championship, Dziwnow Poland:
This was my first Ilca 6 world championship whilst still being quite small for the rig this new sail to me is bigger then the Ilca 4. This regatta was an eye opener to the work I still had to put into this bigger rig to get up to speed at the highest level. Wishing for lighter winds in the qualifying, Poland delivered quite the opposite with a windy three days of qualifying series. Lacking speed paired with some crucial mistakes I ended up in the bronze fleet, my worst world performance to date. This was an important failure to take advantage of as the regatta still isn't over no matter the fleet. I was there to learn as much as I can knowing I am capable of much more. Difficult as it was to swallow, I reset and talked it out with my coach and I was now ready to learn from every opportunity on the race course possible for the next three days. This event was important for me as it showed me yet again how to overcome disappointing outcomes and come back stronger.

Stronger is indeed how I came back in, with a little break back home, I was quickly back on the world stage, this time in  Greece for the Ilca 4 world championship. It was good to be back in the rig I had been training in for the majority of the past year. I was very quickly back up to speed and as the test event for the world championship rolled through I proved to be one of the fastest on the race course. At the same time as recovering from a bronchiis infection, I was however now going into this next world championship with the most confidence in my abilities to date. With my goals and expectations set higher than ever, I proceed to deliver the best performance of my life to date and of any Canadian in the past two decades. A 6th place finish out of 282 boats. A truly unbeleivable week and proof of concept of the process journey I’ve created. I am very proud to have represented Canada to that level. This one deservese a very special thank you to my family, especialy my parents for their unconditional support for this passion, as well as my coach Bernardo Loureiro and all my teamates and trainingparters.

After such a high, the job was not done yet, I headed home to montreal and had one more important target on my sight: the ISAF qualifiying reggatta, this year held that reggata was CORK in kingston. Unfornatly that reggata also did not go amazing, still having slightly lower speed and pace in some conditions as well as some overall not expetional sialing lead t me finish 4th canadian. The good news is i have still two more years two qualify and will continue working my hardest.

That concluded the end of my  2023 season, now was time to go back to the drawing board and plan the season ahead. I spend septemeber and october back at home training from pointclaire yatch and going to the gym as well as competing in the local racing circuit. As winter rolled through, training camps in Cascais (portugal)  and florida started as well as test events throughout spaced out during the winter

I am also happy to announce an exciting year ahead and some challenging goals and events coming up. 

